-- rules/eval-cost.rofl — WHERE A FIXPOINT'S WORK GOES, as a derivation.
--
-- The facts are facts/eval-cost.rofl, regenerated by scanners/eval_cost.ts,
-- which wraps four of `Evaluation`'s methods for the length of one run and
-- restores them afterwards. Nothing is concluded there and nothing is
-- measured here.
--
--   npm run report -- facts/eval-cost.rofl rules/eval-cost.rofl
--   why('barren[main](R)')
--
-- THE MODEL. A rule body is a left-to-right nested loop join. `solveBody`
-- carries an accumulator of partial solutions; at each body position it calls
-- `matchPremise` ONCE PER ACCUMULATED SOLUTION and replaces the accumulator
-- with everything those calls returned. So, per rule and position:
--
--   width(R, I)  = how many partial solutions reached position I
--   yield(R, I)  = how many left it
--   fanout       = yield / width, and width(I+1) = yield(I)
--
-- The work of a firing is the SUM of the widths; the peak is what the space
-- budget watches. And the only quantity here that can EXPLODE is the width,
-- because it is a PRODUCT of the fan-outs before it. That is the whole of
-- cardinality explosion, and it is a property of the ORDER of the body: a
-- fan-out above one at an early position multiplies every position after it,
-- while the same premise last is paid once.

edb(fires).
edb(conc).
edb(fresh).
edb(head).
edb(width).
edb(yield).
edb(slot).
edb(total_width).
edb(total_conc).
edb(total_fresh).
edb(total_rules).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- FIRST: work that produces nothing at all.

work(R, W)   :- width(R, I, N), W is N, I = 0.
touched(R, T) :- width(R, _, T).

-- A rule that was entered, walked solutions, and concluded no head fact. Not
-- an error — a guard rule is allowed to be false — but every one of those
-- solutions was built, unified and thrown away.
barren(R) :- fires(R, F), F > 0, conc(R, 0).

-- How much was spent on it. `wasted` is the width at the position the work
-- actually reached, which is the accumulator that had to exist.
wasted(R, N) :- barren(R), width(R, _, N).

-- AND `wasted` OVERSTATES ITS OWN PRIZE, which is worth deriving rather than
-- remembering. Position 0 is entered ONCE PER FIRING with a single empty
-- solution, so `width(R, 0)` EQUALS `fires(R)` identically and no permutation
-- of any body can move it. The width that ORDER can reduce is everything
-- above position 0. Measured 2026-09-07 on one ring 1 clause: 3799 of 7561
-- elements stand at position 0, and of the 3042 held by the 77 barren rules
-- only 663 are reducible - so ranking by `wasted` promises four and a half
-- times what body order can deliver. Ranking by `reducible` instead puts a
-- rule that CONCLUDES at the top, which `barren` cannot see at all.
reducible(R, I, N) :- width(R, I, N), 0 < I.
order_defect(R)    :- reducible(R, I, N), N > 100.

-- AND `order_defect` IS NOW PARTLY ADDRESSED BY THE EVALUATOR ITSELF, which
-- changes what a reading of it means. `planVersions` gives each semi-naive
-- version of a rule its own body order where the delta can legally lead, so
-- an order that was wrong for ONE version no longer has to be carried by the
-- rest. Measured 2026-09-07 on the same clause: total width 6385 -> 5952, and
-- of the five rules it moves, `spanto` goes 185 -> 79, `punct` 204 -> 105 and

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SECOND: work that produces what is already there.
--
-- `conc` counts head facts BUILT; `fresh` counts the ones the store did not
-- already hold. The difference is re-derivation: a key rendered, a witness
-- offered, and a row the store already had.

redone(R, N)   :- conc(R, C), fresh(R, K), C > K, N is C - K.
all_redone(R)  :- conc(R, C), fresh(R, 0), C > 0.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THIRD: where the width comes from, position by position.

-- A position multiplies when more solutions leave it than entered it.
grows(R, I, F) :- width(R, I, W), yield(R, I, Y), Y > W, F is Y / W.

-- A position filters when far fewer leave than entered.
filters(R, I) :- width(R, I, W), yield(R, I, Y), W > Y.

-- THE ORDER DEFECT, and it is the one the model exists to name. A body that
-- multiplies EARLY and filters LATE pays the multiplication on every position
-- in between. The same two premises in the other order pay it once.
late_filter(R) :- grows(R, I, _), filters(R, J), I < J.

-- The cost of that mistake, stated as the width the filtering position had to
-- receive: everything above what it lets through was built for nothing.
carried(R, N) :- late_filter(R), filters(R, J), width(R, J, N).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- FOURTH: WHICH EDGES ARE WORTH MEASURING.
--
-- This is the part that keeps the next measurement honest. An operation whose
-- total count is a small share of the total width cannot be the reason for
-- anything, and time spent optimising it is time spent on noise — which is
-- exactly the mistake this model was built after making.

share(R, P) :- touched(R, T), total_width(A), P is T * 100 / A.

-- A rule holding at least a twentieth of all the accumulator work in the run.
hot(R)         :- share(R, P), P >= 5.
worth_measuring(R) :- hot(R).
worth_measuring(R) :- barren(R), wasted(R, N), N > 100.
worth_measuring(R) :- all_redone(R), conc(R, C), C > 100.

-- And the negative half, which is the half that gets skipped: a rule below
-- the floor is not worth a profiler, whatever it looks like in a flame graph.
noise(R) :- share(R, P), P < 1, not barren(R).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE ANSWER.

slow_because(fixpoint, barren_rules)  :- barren(R), wasted(R, N), N > 100.
slow_because(fixpoint, late_filter)   :- late_filter(R), carried(R, N), N > 100.
slow_because(fixpoint, re_derivation) :- total_conc(C), total_fresh(K),
                                         D is C - K, E is D * 4, E > C.
